 a cubic polynomial, the term \\( k(x+3)(x-4)(x-5)(x-7) \\) must be a quadratic polynomial, which implies \\( k = 0 \\). Therefore, we can write \\( p(x) = x + 2 \\).\n\nTo verify, we check the given conditions:\n1. \\( p(-3) = -3 + 2 = -1 \\neq -6 \\)\n2. \\( p(4) = 4 + 2 = 6 \\neq 8 \\)\n3. \\( p(5) = 5 + 2 = 7 \\neq 10 \\)\n4. \\( p(7) = 7 + 2 = 9 \\neq 15 \\)\n\nThis suggests that our initial assumption \\( p(x) = x + 2 \\) is incorrect, and we need to find the correct cubic polynomial. Let's define a new polynomial \\( q(x) = p(x) - (x+2) \\). Then \\( q(x) \\) is a cubic polynomial with roots at \\( x = -3, 4, 5, 7 \\), but since it is cubic, it must be of the form \\( q(x) = k(x+3)(x-4)(x-5) \\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[ p(x) = k(x+3)(x-4)(x-5) + x + 2. \\]\n\nWe need to determine the value of \\( k \\). Using the condition \\( p(7) = 15 \\):\n\\[ p(7) = k(7+3)(7-4)(7-5) + 7 + 2 = 15k + 9 = 15. \\]\nSolving for \\( k \\):\n\\[ 15k + 9 = 15 \\]\n\\[ 15k = 6 \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{2}{5}. \\]\nSo, the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is:\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{2}{5}(x+3)(x-4)(x-5) + x + 2. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\( p(12) \\):\n\\[ p(12) = \\frac{2}{5}(12+3)(12-4)(12-5) + 12 + 2. \\]\nSimplify inside the parentheses:\n\\[ p(12) = \\frac{2}{5}(15)(8)(7) + 14. \\]\nCalculate the product:\n\\[ p(12) = \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot 840 + 14 = \\frac{1680}{5} + 14 = 336 + 14 = 350. \\]\nThus, the value of \\( p(12) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{350}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{350}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:150"}
